    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s1",
      "source_id": "0bc1e171-1f42-4831-86cf-a4338868db05",
      "text": "When things move from \"planned\" to \"emergency,\" the goal is immediate, immediate stability. In a last-minute emergency, you don’t have time for \"research,\" so you have to pivot to \"survival mode.\"\n\nHere is the \"last-minute emergency\" strategy:\n\n1.  **The \"Rule of 3s\":** Instead of trying to pack a full survival kit (which takes time), look for the most immediate, small-scale fixes:\n    *   **Immediate Power:** If you don't have power, look for the nearest public \"power-sharing\" spot (a library, a cafe, or a 24-hour \"charging station\" like a hotel lobby or airport lounge).\n    *   **The \"Emergency Swap\":** If your primary gear breaks, look for the absolute cheapest, most basic \"replacement\" version of that item. For example, if your primary phone dies, use the phone's built-in *emergency* emergency mode on a different network.\n2.  **\"The Quick Buy\":** If the trip is \"last-minute,\" you don't have time to plan a long-term strategy. Instead, focus on the **most essential, single-use"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s2",
      "source_id": "fbe23198-383e-47b2-8fc9-c73ffb2b61f2",
      "text": "What if you don't have access to those public spots? Like, you're in a remote area with no cafes or libraries nearby?"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s3",
      "source_id": "8416f607-0438-4818-941f-21a07a1c7e9e",
      "text": "If you're completely cut off from those standard public charging spots, you're looking at a \"deep survival\" scenario. At this point, you have to rely on your **immediate physical inventory** and your **last-known location data**.\n\nHere's the breakdown for when you are \"off-grid\":\n\n*   **The \"Low-Tech\" Backup:** You need the \"low-tech\" survival kit. If the power is gone, your phone is useless. Look for **physical analog solutions**. This means physical cash, physical maps/print-outs if available, or simple physical tools (e.g., a compass/map if the trip is outdoors).\n*   **The \"Borrowed Energy\" Strategy:** If you can’t charge, you need to find a **source of chemical energy**. In a true emergency, you look for the most basic local resources: a friendly stranger or a local service that might offer a 5-minute charge, even if you have to pay for it.\n*   **The \"Human-to-Human\" Link:** Since digital tools are down, your only remaining resource is human connection. Talk to the people nearby. Ask the closest local authority or community member for immediate, small, non"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "external",
      "slot": "s4",
      "source_id": "beb01fee-4922-4d3a-83d0-22e051a0d710",
      "text": "What if you're in a place where even the basics like cash or maps aren't reliable? Like, you're in a foreign country with no local contacts and your phone dies?"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s5",
      "source_id": "600ac620-aa50-4ad5-9daa-f9322f6aa332",
      "text": "In a situation where all digital and physical safety nets (phone, cash, local maps) have failed, you shift into \"Deep Analog Mode.\" You are operating purely on physical resilience and local knowledge.\n\nHere is how you handle \"Deep Survival Mode\" in that specific scenario:\n\n*   **The \"Self-Sufficient\" Principle:** You stop trying to use *technology* and start using *physical location.* If your phone is dead, you must use a **physical map/compass** (if available) or a **local, known-to-be-stable location** (a landmark, like a river or a specific mountain peak). You are no longer trying to \"solve a problem\"; you are *finding a way out*.\n*   **The \"Human Bridge\":** Since you can't rely on digital communication or financial tools, you look for the **\"Human-to-Human Bridge.\"** This means finding a trusted, high-level local authority—a police station, a religious center, or an established local business/community center—to find someone who has *physical* knowledge or resources.\n*   **The \"Patience Protocol\":** In this scenario, you stop trying to \"fix\" the system. You stop trying"
    }
